From cc80a8cbaf4f61d746ba622fdd4c6ac40632fffd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:42:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/14] ci: import every module of each package from a fresh
 virtualenv

The python job installs every package plus requirements-dev.txt into
one environment, so a package that imports a module it does not
declare still passes: a sibling package or a dev dependency provides
it. That is how forgemesh-cli shipped without pyyaml and the control
plane, and how control-plane and plugin-sdk missed dependencies
before.

scripts/check_package_imports.py installs each package of packages/
into its own new virtualenv with only its declared dependencies (and
all of its own extras), then imports the root module and every
submodule found by pkgutil.walk_packages with python -I, so neither
PYTHONPATH nor the working directory can supply a module. Local
forgemesh-* dependencies are built from packages/ (on a copy, so no
stale build/lib leaks into the wheels); a forgemesh-* dependency that
is not in packages/ is an error instead of a lookup on the package
index. A package fails if any import fails or if the imported modules
differ from the source tree. Third-party dependencies come from the
package index, so the check runs in a new package-imports CI job
rather than in pytest; the unit tests cover the parts that need no
network.
